<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow"><title>Paper B · Learner paper</title><base href="/api/v1/ap-chinese/2027/b/file/"><link rel="stylesheet" href="paper.css"><body><main class="page teacher"><section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">HSKKit · AP Chinese 2027 · Original practice</p><h1>Paper B · Learner paper</h1><p class="lead">55 MCQs + four free-response tasks. Speaking uses an independent music-research scenario; the official 2027 project packet is not yet released.</p><div class="links"><a href="index.html">Practice workspace</a><a href="student.pdf">Learner PDF · 简体</a><a href="student-traditional.pdf">Learner PDF · 繁體</a><a href="teacher.pdf">Answer PDF</a><a href="listening.html">Audio review</a></div></div></section><div class="box"><h2>Before speaking: prepare your own research</h2><p>Before speaking, investigate how a musical tradition in a Chinese-speaking community is learned or passed to new participants. Consult at least two identifiable sources. Connect a specific learning practice with a cultural perspective, and distinguish documented information from your interpretation. The source leads below support independent research; they are not an official course-project packet.</p><p><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/00202">UNESCO: Grand song of the Dong ethnic group</a></p><p class="cn">UNESCO 的侗族大歌条目介绍了没有乐器伴奏或指挥的多声部歌唱，也说明歌师向合唱群体中的学习者传授歌曲。可继续研究：演出时不设指挥，与学习时接受指导有什么不同？共同聆听、记忆和配合可能怎样参与传承？后一个问题是研究方向，不是对所有参与者感受的既定结论。</p><p><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/xi-an-wind-and-percussion-ensemble-00212">UNESCO: Xi’an wind and percussion ensemble</a></p><p class="cn">UNESCO 的西安鼓乐条目介绍了鼓与管乐器结合的音乐形式，提到师徒传承，也提到保存并使用的手写曲谱。可继续研究：曲谱、示范和群体练习各能帮助新手理解什么？不要仅凭一种记录方式，就判断所有学习者的能力或所有当代团体的教学做法。</p></div><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 1</p><h2>Project presentation</h2><p>Present your research on how a musical tradition in a Chinese-speaking community is learned or passed on. Explain a specific learning practice and its relationship to a cultural perspective. Use identifiable evidence, include a comparison or reflection, and discuss one limit of what your sources establish. Speak in Chinese.</p><p class="cn">请介绍你研究的一种华语文化情境中的音乐传统怎样被学习或传承。说明一种具体学习实践及其与文化观念的关系，结合可辨认的资料证据，作适当比较或反思，并指出来源所能说明的问题有哪些局限。请用中文作答。</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 11 points maximum: 任务完成 2, 文化理解 2, 组织 2, 语言控制 3, 表达呈现 2. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 2</p><h2>Project Q&A</h2><p>Answer four questions in Chinese about your research into learning and transmitting a musical tradition. Each question is heard twice, followed by a forty-second response window. The whole four-response exchange receives one analytic review.</p><p class="muted">Four recorded questions. Each prompt plays twice, then allows 40 seconds for a response. Open the practice workspace or audio review to hear them.</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 9 points maximum: 任务完成 2, 文化理解 2, 语言控制 3, 流利度 2. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 3</p><h2>Story narration</h2><p>Write a complete story in Chinese using all four pictures in order. Explain what happens before, during, and after the problem. You may choose names and add plausible details that fit the sequence.</p><p class="cn">请按顺序使用全部四幅图，用中文写一个完整故事，交代问题出现前、发生时和解决后的情节。可以自定人物姓名，并补充符合画面顺序的合理细节。</p><div class="story-grid"><figure><img src="assets/images/story-1.svg" alt="Two volunteers load four potted seedlings onto a cart beside the community garden."><figcaption>Picture 1</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-2.svg" alt="A wheel comes off the loaded cart; the volunteers notice the problem before moving farther."><figcaption>Picture 2</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-3.svg" alt="The volunteers lift the seedling tray together and carry it toward the planting beds."><figcaption>Picture 3</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-4.svg" alt="The seedlings are planted in the garden beds; the empty tray and stopped cart are set aside."><figcaption>Picture 4</figcaption></figure></div><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 8 points maximum: 叙事展开 3, 组织 2, 语言控制 3. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 4</p><h2>Email response</h2><p>Reply in Chinese to the coordinator’s email. Address each request and ask a relevant preparation question. Use an appropriate greeting, organization, and closing.</p><p class="cn">学校交流办公室｜学生版校园指南
同学你好！下个月有一组短期交流学生来校，我们想请学生编一份简短的双语校园指南。你认为最值得加入哪类信息？请说明理由，以及你愿意负责的工作。
有些信息，例如活动时间和联系人，可能会变化。你建议怎样保持资料可靠？纸面篇幅有限，但读者的中文水平不同，你会怎样兼顾清楚和简短？最后，请提出一个你开始准备前需要向办公室确认的问题。
期待你的建议！
协调员 周老师</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 8 points maximum: 信息展开 3, 组织 2, 语言控制 3. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L1 · Questions 1–5</p><h2>Reaching an island lecture on time</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l1.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>周六上午开往海岛的客船</caption><thead><tr><th>出发码头</th><th>开船时间</th><th>抵达时间</th><th>单程票价</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>滨海码头</td><td>08:40</td><td>09:20</td><td>30元</td></tr><tr><td>河口码头</td><td>08:30</td><td>09:40</td><td>18元</td></tr><tr><td>滨海码头</td><td>09:20</td><td>10:00</td><td>30元</td></tr><tr><td>河口码头</td><td>10:10</td><td>11:20</td><td>18元</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">航班与价格均为本卷原创模拟资料。</p><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l1.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>週六上午開往海島的客船</caption><thead><tr><th>出發碼頭</th><th>開船時間</th><th>抵達時間</th><th>單程票價</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>濱海碼頭</td><td>08:40</td><td>09:20</td><td>30元</td></tr><tr><td>河口碼頭</td><td>08:30</td><td>09:40</td><td>18元</td></tr><tr><td>濱海碼頭</td><td>09:20</td><td>10:00</td><td>30元</td></tr><tr><td>河口碼頭</td><td>10:10</td><td>11:20</td><td>18元</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">航班與價格均爲本卷原創模擬資料。</p></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>1. Which departure fits Chen’s stated budget and the registration deadline?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The 08:40 departure from Coastal Pier.</p><p><b>B.</b> The 09:20 departure from Coastal Pier.</p><p><b>C.</b> The 10:10 departure from River Mouth Pier.</p><p><b>D.</b> The 08:30 departure from River Mouth Pier.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>2. For the 08:30 River Mouth departure, when must ticket purchase be completed?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> By 08:30 at the automatic ticket machine.</p><p><b>B.</b> By 08:15 at the staffed counter.</p><p><b>C.</b> By 09:40 after arriving on the island.</p><p><b>D.</b> By 09:50 at the lecture registration desk.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>3. What does the listed fare cover?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Both the outward and return crossings.</p><p><b>B.</b> The crossing and all transport on the island.</p><p><b>C.</b> Only the one-way boat journey shown in the table.</p><p><b>D.</b> The lecture admission and a reserved return sailing.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>4. How should travelers interpret a rain symbol on a weather app?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Check the pier’s operational notice before assuming a cancellation.</p><p><b>B.</b> Assume all sailings are cancelled regardless of any pier notice.</p><p><b>C.</b> Choose the most expensive departure to avoid weather restrictions.</p><p><b>D.</b> Treat the return sailing time as unchanged without checking it.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>5. Why does the speaker discuss several steps beyond the crossing itself?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To persuade everyone to choose the fastest and most expensive route.</p><p><b>B.</b> To show that registration can be completed at any time after the lecture.</p><p><b>C.</b> To imply that walking time is included in the chart’s arrival time.</p><p><b>D.</b> To help students plan a feasible journey rather than just select a sailing.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L2 · Questions 6–10</p><h2>A neighborhood tests a night-lighting plan</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l2.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l2.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>6. What is the report primarily describing?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A trial that adjusts decorative lighting while responding to different users.</p><p><b>B.</b> A plan to remove all lighting from the commercial street after sunset.</p><p><b>C.</b> A competition to choose the brightest shop sign in the neighborhood.</p><p><b>D.</b> A decision to make every shop remain open through the night.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>7. Which lighting is explicitly left unchanged in the initial trial?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> All lights directed toward residential windows.</p><p><b>B.</b> Every decorative lamp outside shops.</p><p><b>C.</b> Basic lighting at bus stops and along pedestrian paths.</p><p><b>D.</b> Only the illuminated lettering on commercial signs.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>8. What does the night-shift worker’s comment add to the discussion?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Evidence that no residents go to sleep before eleven.</p><p><b>B.</b> A reminder that less common schedules still create legitimate needs.</p><p><b>C.</b> A request to measure electricity only during shop opening hours.</p><p><b>D.</b> Proof that every resident wants the same lighting level.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>9. Why is a low pedestrian count on one night not enough to remove a route’s lighting?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It proves that the route is used only by shop owners.</p><p><b>B.</b> It means the electricity meter cannot record that night.</p><p><b>C.</b> It shows that all feedback must be collected online.</p><p><b>D.</b> It does not establish that the route has no important users.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>10. How does the project leader propose evaluating the changes?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Combine electricity records with location-specific feedback.</p><p><b>B.</b> Choose whichever shop uses the least electricity as the sole model.</p><p><b>C.</b> Stop revising the plan once a majority has voted.</p><p><b>D.</b> Treat a reduction in power use as proof that every route is convenient.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L3 · Questions 11–15</p><h2>What does an invitation to participate really mean?</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l3.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l3.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>11. What problem does the male speaker identify in the proposed translation?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It makes the club sound as if it offers only technical roles.</p><p><b>B.</b> It suggests that performances will take place without a stage.</p><p><b>C.</b> It turns an invitation to participate into an obligation to perform.</p><p><b>D.</b> It gives rehearsal times that conflict with the Chinese poster.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>12. Which example would best reassure the student who is nervous about speaking?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A beginner can try a backstage role with guidance.</p><p><b>B.</b> Every participant must deliver a speech at the first rehearsal.</p><p><b>C.</b> Only experienced performers may attend the open rehearsal.</p><p><b>D.</b> Signing up removes all expectations about attendance.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>13. In this conversation, what does “门槛不高” suggest?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The entrance to the theater has been physically lowered.</p><p><b>B.</b> The performance space has a limited number of seats.</p><p><b>C.</b> The club’s budget is smaller than last year’s budget.</p><p><b>D.</b> A high level of prior experience is not required.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>14. What qualification do the speakers add to the idea of free choice?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Participants should choose roles solely according to the shortest title.</p><p><b>B.</b> Choosing a role still requires meeting stated commitments.</p><p><b>C.</b> Every participant may change the performance date independently.</p><p><b>D.</b> The English poster should omit scheduling details to stay brief.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>15. What broader approach to translation do the speakers support?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Follow the literal wording even if readers misunderstand the invitation.</p><p><b>B.</b> Use the shortest possible phrase regardless of missing information.</p><p><b>C.</b> Communicate the intended choices and responsibilities in context.</p><p><b>D.</b> Remove the Chinese slogan because figurative language cannot be explained.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L4 · Questions 16–20</p><h2>Preparing a map for a digital exhibition</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l4.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l4.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>16. Why should the map’s recording date appear near its title?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To make clear that the map documents a past situation.</p><p><b>B.</b> To guarantee that every street still follows the same layout.</p><p><b>C.</b> To replace the need to provide a title for the exhibition.</p><p><b>D.</b> To indicate the exact time every future visitor will arrive.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>17. What should be done before reducing the size of a scanned map?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Delete the original so the computer uses less storage.</p><p><b>B.</b> Publish the largest version and resize it only after complaints.</p><p><b>C.</b> Create an upload copy while retaining the original file.</p><p><b>D.</b> Remove all small writing regardless of its meaning.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>18. What would best satisfy the instruction about the red and green routes?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Make both routes thinner and leave the legend blank.</p><p><b>B.</b> Use different line patterns or labels as well as color.</p><p><b>C.</b> Choose darker colors without adding any other distinction.</p><p><b>D.</b> Explain only that one color is more attractive than the other.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>19. What is the correct response if the legend was cut off during scanning?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Assume visitors can infer the symbols from the title alone.</p><p><b>B.</b> Hide all route lines to avoid explaining their meaning.</p><p><b>C.</b> Add an unrelated sample legend copied from another map.</p><p><b>D.</b> Scan the complete page again so the original legend is included.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>20. What is the purpose of the draft stage after submission?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It lets the author check the presentation before making it public.</p><p><b>B.</b> It proves the map will automatically update as streets change.</p><p><b>C.</b> It allows the original file to be discarded without consequence.</p><p><b>D.</b> It prevents the author from correcting links once they are entered.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L5 · Questions 21–25</p><h2>What a household task chart could not count</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l5.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l5.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>21. What does the speaker’s experience suggest about the original point system?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It accurately measured every kind of contribution from the beginning.</p><p><b>B.</b> It made visible results comparable while overlooking important differences.</p><p><b>C.</b> It failed because the family refused to record any completed tasks.</p><p><b>D.</b> It showed that preparation takes no time and needs no recognition.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>22. Which contribution illustrates the work that timing alone initially missed?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Recording the exact number of dishes after washing.</p><p><b>B.</b> Comparing who accumulated the highest monthly total.</p><p><b>C.</b> Watching the clock while another person shops.</p><p><b>D.</b> Anticipating what the household will need during the week.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>23. Why does the family ask the sister to accompany her grandfather to the market?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To help her learn a responsibility rather than fixing her permanently in one task.</p><p><b>B.</b> To prove that only older people can understand how to buy food.</p><p><b>C.</b> To replace all planning with a rule that everyone buys the same items.</p><p><b>D.</b> To remove her from the discussion of how household work is shared.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>24. In the final part, what does “搭把手” mean?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Record another person’s mistakes on the household chart.</p><p><b>B.</b> Complete every task without consulting anyone else.</p><p><b>C.</b> Offer practical help when another person needs support.</p><p><b>D.</b> Let the person with the most points set all the rules.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>25. What is the speaker’s final position on task charts?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A chart should replace conversation once every box has been filled in.</p><p><b>B.</b> A chart can be useful if it supports discussion and changing needs.</p><p><b>C.</b> A chart can guarantee the same arrangement will suit every household.</p><p><b>D.</b> A chart is useful only when the family stops making changes to it.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R1 · Questions 26–30</p><h2>Revising a language-partner program</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 邮件一｜协调员致学生志愿者
各位同学：下个月的语言伙伴计划原定每周二晚上见面一小时，整个学期固定搭档。报名者中有人想练习日常交谈，也有人主要想了解课堂讨论的表达方式。我们准备让每次活动都按同一份话题表进行，方便统一管理。请大家周五前提出意见，并说明自己能负责的工作。</p><p><b>p2</b> 邮件二｜志愿者顾岚回复
老师您好！固定时间和搭档有助于熟悉彼此，不过有几位报名者周二要实习，照这个安排，他们还没开始就会被排除。我建议保留周二活动，同时增加一个可预约的周末小组。搭档可以先固定两次，再根据双方目标协商是否调整，而不是每次都随机换人。</p><p><b>p3</b> 话题表可以作为备用，不必要求每个人按顺序谈完。日常交谈组可用具体生活情境，课堂讨论组则可以练习提问和说明理由。我可以整理两类活动卡，并在首次见面时帮助大家写下自己的目标。是否能先试行四周，再收集参与者对安排的反馈？另外，我想确认，周末活动室是否已经有人负责开门。这个问题解决后，我就能联系小组成员。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 郵件一｜協調員致學生志願者
各位同學：下個月的語言夥伴計劃原定每週二晚上見面一小時，整個學期固定搭檔。報名者中有人想練習日常交談，也有人主要想了解課堂討論的表達方式。我們準備讓每次活動都按同一份話題表進行，方便統一管理。請大家週五前提出意見，並說明自己能負責的工作。</p><p><b>p2</b> 郵件二｜志願者顧嵐回覆
老師您好！固定時間和搭檔有助於熟悉彼此，不過有幾位報名者週二要實習，照這個安排，他們還沒開始就會被排除。我建議保留週二活動，同時增加一個可預約的週末小組。搭檔可以先固定兩次，再根據雙方目標協商是否調整，而不是每次都隨機換人。</p><p><b>p3</b> 話題表可以作爲備用，不必要求每個人按順序談完。日常交談組可用具體生活情境，課堂討論組則可以練習提問和說明理由。我可以整理兩類活動卡，並在首次見面時幫助大家寫下自己的目標。是否能先試行四周，再收集參與者對安排的反饋？另外，我想確認，週末活動室是否已經有人負責開門。這個問題解決後，我就能聯繫小組成員。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>26. What is Gu Lan’s main purpose in replying?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To suggest a more flexible plan while offering specific help.</p><p><b>B.</b> To ask that all organized meetings be replaced by unscheduled encounters.</p><p><b>C.</b> To reject both fixed partners and any record of participants’ goals.</p><p><b>D.</b> To confirm that every applicant has already agreed to Tuesday evenings.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>27. What does Gu propose for matching partners?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Assign a different partner randomly at every meeting.</p><p><b>B.</b> Keep the original partner for the entire term regardless of goals.</p><p><b>C.</b> Let only the coordinator decide changes without consulting participants.</p><p><b>D.</b> Keep partners initially, then discuss changes based on their aims.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>28. Why does Gu suggest a weekend group?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The Tuesday room is permanently unavailable.</p><p><b>B.</b> The topic cards cannot be used in evening sessions.</p><p><b>C.</b> Some interested applicants have commitments on Tuesdays.</p><p><b>D.</b> The participants have requested that every meeting become longer.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>29. How would the topic list function under Gu’s proposal?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> As a required sequence that every pair must complete.</p><p><b>B.</b> As optional support adapted to different learning goals.</p><p><b>C.</b> As a test that ranks participants before assigning partners.</p><p><b>D.</b> As a replacement for discussing what participants hope to practice.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>30. What needs to be confirmed before Gu contacts the weekend group?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Whether every Tuesday applicant can move to weekends.</p><p><b>B.</b> Whether a new term-long fixed pairing list has been approved.</p><p><b>C.</b> Whether someone is responsible for opening the activity room.</p><p><b>D.</b> Whether all daily-life cards have been translated into every language.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R2 · Questions 31–35</p><h2>More than a smaller portion</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 晴川中学食堂过去只有一种饭菜分量。几位学生反映，有时吃不完，却不好意思提出少盛一点。食堂于是试行普通份和小份两种选择，价格相同，小份吃完仍可以免费添饭。工作人员希望先让学生选择，再了解剩餐变化，而不是统一把每份都缩小。</p><p><b>p2</b> 表中记录了试行前一周和试行两周的每日平均数据。“剩餐”只计算用餐后盘中未吃完的食物，不包括厨房备餐时产生的部分。第二周参加校内活动的人增加，供餐数比前一周多；各周菜单也不完全相同。</p><p><b>p3</b> 试行第二周的剩餐总量比第一周上升，有人因此说方案失败了。学生观察组认为，不能忽略供餐人数的变化；与试行前相比，第二周供餐更多，剩餐仍更少。不过，这些数据还不能证明每一种菜都会同样减少浪费。小组建议继续记录，并分别询问选择小份和普通份的学生，看看添饭是否方便、分量是否合适。</p><p><b>p4</b> 一位食堂工作人员说，如果只是把剩餐数字降下来，却让学生不敢添饭，就偏离了目的。下一轮会把添饭窗口标识做得更清楚，并保留两种分量，让“吃多少盛多少”成为可以实际选择的做法。</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>晴川中学食堂三周记录</caption><thead><tr><th>时段</th><th>每日平均供餐数</th><th>每日平均剩餐</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>试行前一周</td><td>400份</td><td>18千克</td></tr><tr><td>试行第一周</td><td>400份</td><td>12千克</td></tr><tr><td>试行第二周</td><td>480份</td><td>15千克</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">本表为原创模拟数据。各周均按实际开放日计算平均值；并非对所有学校的调查。</p><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 晴川中學食堂過去只有一種飯菜分量。幾位學生反映，有時喫不完，卻不好意思提出少盛一點。食堂於是試行普通份和小份兩種選擇，價格相同，小份喫完仍可以免費添飯。工作人員希望先讓學生選擇，再瞭解剩餐變化，而不是統一把每份都縮小。</p><p><b>p2</b> 表中記錄了試行前一週和試行兩週的每日平均數據。“剩餐”只計算用餐後盤中未喫完的食物，不包括廚房備餐時產生的部分。第二週參加校內活動的人增加，供餐數比前一週多；各周菜單也不完全相同。</p><p><b>p3</b> 試行第二週的剩餐總量比第一週上升，有人因此說方案失敗了。學生觀察組認爲，不能忽略供餐人數的變化；與試行前相比，第二週供餐更多，剩餐仍更少。不過，這些數據還不能證明每一種菜都會同樣減少浪費。小組建議繼續記錄，並分別詢問選擇小份和普通份的學生，看看添飯是否方便、分量是否合適。</p><p><b>p4</b> 一位食堂工作人員說，如果只是把剩餐數字降下來，卻讓學生不敢添飯，就偏離了目的。下一輪會把添飯窗口標識做得更清楚，並保留兩種分量，讓“喫多少盛多少”成爲可以實際選擇的做法。</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>晴川中學食堂三週記錄</caption><thead><tr><th>時段</th><th>每日平均供餐數</th><th>每日平均剩餐</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>試行前一週</td><td>400份</td><td>18千克</td></tr><tr><td>試行第一週</td><td>400份</td><td>12千克</td></tr><tr><td>試行第二週</td><td>480份</td><td>15千克</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">本表爲原創模擬數據。各周均按實際開放日計算平均值；並非對所有學校的調查。</p></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>31. What distinguishes the trial from simply reducing every meal’s size?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Everyone must choose a small portion and pay to add more.</p><p><b>B.</b> Students can choose a portion and add food without an extra charge.</p><p><b>C.</b> The school has removed all standard portions from the menu.</p><p><b>D.</b> Kitchen preparation waste is counted as a student’s unfinished meal.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>32. What does the reported “leftover food” measure include?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> All waste produced by the school throughout the day.</p><p><b>B.</b> Only unused ingredients left in the kitchen after preparation.</p><p><b>C.</b> The weight of meals students bought but had not yet collected.</p><p><b>D.</b> Food remaining on diners’ plates after they have eaten.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>33. Which comparison is directly supported by the table?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The second trial week served fewer meals than before the trial.</p><p><b>B.</b> The first trial week produced the same leftovers as the baseline week.</p><p><b>C.</b> The second trial week served more meals but left less food than the baseline week.</p><p><b>D.</b> The two trial weeks had identical meal counts and leftover totals.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>34. Why can these three weeks not establish the same effect for every dish?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The menus and attendance conditions were not identical across weeks.</p><p><b>B.</b> The observers refused to count the number of meals served.</p><p><b>C.</b> The table combines measurements taken at different schools.</p><p><b>D.</b> The trial did not offer students any real choice of portion.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>35. What would best match the staff member’s understanding of success?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Discourage additional servings so that the waste total is always low.</p><p><b>B.</b> Close the standard-portion line to make everyone’s plate identical.</p><p><b>C.</b> Reward only the week with the lowest total regardless of attendance.</p><p><b>D.</b> Make portion choices and additional servings clear and usable.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R3 · Questions 36–40</p><h2>When one missing word changes a caption</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 校刊科技组第一次试用课堂自动字幕时，给出的结论很简单：一百个字只错一个，已经足够可靠。后来他们发现，字幕把老师说的“不要马上交卷”漏掉了一个“不”字，剩下的字虽然都对，学生理解的行动却完全相反。</p><p><b>p2</b> 小组于是改变了检查方式。除了记录错字数，他们还把人名、数字、否定词和转折关系单独标出来，看看错误是否改变主要信息。同样错一个字，姓名中的错字可能使人认错对象，普通语气词的差异却未必影响任务。一个总分不能说明所有错误的重要程度。</p><p><b>p3</b> 有同学建议直接把字幕全部改得通顺，再公开最终版本。编辑认为，这样读起来方便，但会让后来的使用者看不见哪里曾经不确定。最后，展示页面保留原始字幕，并用另一栏列出核对后的版本，注明修改依据。遇到没有听清的地方，不为了版面完整而补写一个看似合理的词，而是回听录音、请说话者确认，或者保留“待核实”的标记。</p><p><b>p4</b> 这次试用只涉及两堂课，课堂讨论的声音条件和正式讲台讲话也不同。小组不再用“准确率很高，所以处处都能放心用”作结，而把使用场景、检查方法和仍未解决的问题写进报告。他们认为，技术工具有价值，但负责任的使用也包括知道什么时候需要停下来核对。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 校刊科技組第一次試用課堂自動字幕時，給出的結論很簡單：一百個字只錯一個，已經足夠可靠。後來他們發現，字幕把老師說的“不要馬上交卷”漏掉了一個“不”字，剩下的字雖然都對，學生理解的行動卻完全相反。</p><p><b>p2</b> 小組於是改變了檢查方式。除了記錄錯字數，他們還把人名、數字、否定詞和轉折關係單獨標出來，看看錯誤是否改變主要信息。同樣錯一個字，姓名中的錯字可能使人認錯對象，普通語氣詞的差異卻未必影響任務。一個總分不能說明所有錯誤的重要程度。</p><p><b>p3</b> 有同學建議直接把字幕全部改得通順，再公開最終版本。編輯認爲，這樣讀起來方便，但會讓後來的使用者看不見哪裏曾經不確定。最後，展示頁面保留原始字幕，並用另一欄列出覈對後的版本，註明修改依據。遇到沒有聽清的地方，不爲了版面完整而補寫一個看似合理的詞，而是回聽錄音、請說話者確認，或者保留“待覈實”的標記。</p><p><b>p4</b> 這次試用只涉及兩堂課，課堂討論的聲音條件和正式講臺講話也不同。小組不再用“準確率很高，所以處處都能放心用”作結，而把使用場景、檢查方法和仍未解決的問題寫進報告。他們認爲，技術工具有價值，但負責任的使用也包括知道什麼時候需要停下來覈對。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>36. How does the group’s evaluation of automatic captions change?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It shifts from checking meaning to judging only the elegance of sentences.</p><p><b>B.</b> It stops considering any error once the overall accuracy seems high.</p><p><b>C.</b> It treats every wrong character as equally important in every situation.</p><p><b>D.</b> It begins examining how particular errors affect the intended information.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>37. What does the missing “不” in the opening example demonstrate?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A subtitle can be useful only if every speaker uses exactly one sentence.</p><p><b>B.</b> A single omission can reverse the action a listener is asked to take.</p><p><b>C.</b> Negation words have no effect on the practical meaning of instructions.</p><p><b>D.</b> Counting correct characters always identifies the most serious misunderstanding.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>38. Why does the final page keep an original caption column alongside corrections?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> So readers can trace uncertainty and the basis of revisions.</p><p><b>B.</b> So the group can avoid explaining why any change was made.</p><p><b>C.</b> So every original error will be treated as the speaker’s intended meaning.</p><p><b>D.</b> So a fluent final sentence can replace evidence from the recording.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>39. If a word cannot be heard clearly, which action follows the group’s approach?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Insert the most fluent guess and remove all signs of doubt.</p><p><b>B.</b> Copy a likely word from an unrelated class’s transcript.</p><p><b>C.</b> Seek confirmation or leave the uncertainty explicitly marked.</p><p><b>D.</b> Assume the word is unimportant because only one character is missing.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>40. Which statement would be most appropriate in the group’s final report?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The two-class trial proves the same performance in every speaking environment.</p><p><b>B.</b> A polished final transcript makes the original conditions irrelevant.</p><p><b>C.</b> High overall accuracy eliminates the need to identify remaining problems.</p><p><b>D.</b> The observed performance should be interpreted with its setting and checks described.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R4 · Questions 41–45</p><h2>A guide’s blank page</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 第一次在学生画展做讲解，我把每幅画的介绍背得很熟，连停顿都写在纸上。我最怕有人突然提问，因为一旦离开准备好的句子，就不知道该说什么。开场前，老师只对我说：“别急着把所有空白填满。”我以为她嫌我写得太多。</p><p><b>p2</b> 讲到一幅画着半开门的作品时，一个孩子问：“门后面的人为什么不出来？”我的纸上只有作者、尺寸和创作年份，没有这个问题的答案。我差点随口编一个故事，看到孩子认真等着，又停住了。我说：“我没有问过画这幅画的人，不能替他回答。你觉得门后面会是什么？”</p><p><b>p3</b> 孩子说，也许有人正在等朋友。旁边一位阿姨却想到，门半开着，可能是一个人想出去又犹豫。大家开始指着画里的光线讨论，原来安静跟着走的观众也凑近了一些。我把讲解纸翻到背面，在空白处记下他们的话，忽然不再急着接回原来的段落。</p><p><b>p4</b> 结束后，我仍然把作品资料查得很仔细，却在每段介绍旁留出一块空白。那不是忘记准备，而是提醒自己：有些事实需要准确说明，有些问题可以一起想。下一次讲解前，我的纸比以前空了，心里反而没有那么慌。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 第一次在學生畫展做講解，我把每幅畫的介紹背得很熟，連停頓都寫在紙上。我最怕有人突然提問，因爲一旦離開準備好的句子，就不知道該說什麼。開場前，老師只對我說：“別急着把所有空白填滿。”我以爲她嫌我寫得太多。</p><p><b>p2</b> 講到一幅畫着半開門的作品時，一個孩子問：“門後面的人爲什麼不出來？”我的紙上只有作者、尺寸和創作年份，沒有這個問題的答案。我差點隨口編一個故事，看到孩子認真等着，又停住了。我說：“我沒有問過畫這幅畫的人，不能替他回答。你覺得門後面會是什麼？”</p><p><b>p3</b> 孩子說，也許有人正在等朋友。旁邊一位阿姨卻想到，門半開着，可能是一個人想出去又猶豫。大家開始指着畫裏的光線討論，原來安靜跟着走的觀衆也湊近了一些。我把講解紙翻到背面，在空白處記下他們的話，忽然不再急着接回原來的段落。</p><p><b>p4</b> 結束後，我仍然把作品資料查得很仔細，卻在每段介紹旁留出一塊空白。那不是忘記準備，而是提醒自己：有些事實需要準確說明，有些問題可以一起想。下一次講解前，我的紙比以前空了，心裏反而沒有那麼慌。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>41. What makes the narrator anxious at the beginning?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The exhibition has no labels identifying any of the artists.</p><p><b>B.</b> The teacher has forbidden the use of notes during the tour.</p><p><b>C.</b> An unexpected question might take the narrator beyond the rehearsed script.</p><p><b>D.</b> Visitors have been told that every guide must invent a story for each painting.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>42. Why is the narrator’s reply to the child a turning point?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It acknowledges a limit of knowledge and opens a shared discussion.</p><p><b>B.</b> It establishes that the child’s interpretation is the painter’s confirmed intention.</p><p><b>C.</b> It shows that dates and dimensions can answer every question about art.</p><p><b>D.</b> It prevents the other visitors from expressing a different view.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>43. What does writing on the back of the paper reveal about the narrator?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The narrator has decided that the original factual research was worthless.</p><p><b>B.</b> The narrator wants to replace all visitor comments with a single official answer.</p><p><b>C.</b> The narrator is trying to hide the painting from further questions.</p><p><b>D.</b> The narrator is beginning to treat visitors’ contributions as part of the experience.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>44. How does the final paragraph reinterpret the blank spaces in the notes?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> As proof that preparation is unnecessary for any guide.</p><p><b>B.</b> As deliberate room for inquiry alongside accurate information.</p><p><b>C.</b> As a method for making visitors finish the guide’s homework.</p><p><b>D.</b> As evidence that factual descriptions should be replaced by guesses.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>45. What relationship between a guide and an audience does the story favor?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The audience should remain silent whenever a guide has prepared enough material.</p><p><b>B.</b> The guide should agree that all interpretations are established historical facts.</p><p><b>C.</b> Accurate guidance can coexist with listening and open-ended participation.</p><p><b>D.</b> A guide should answer every question immediately even without evidence.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R5 · Questions 46–50</p><h2>A walking route with room for everyone</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 城市慢行工作室｜街角发现计划
周日上午九点至十一点，从青石广场出发。我们不比谁走得快，而是一起观察日常经过却没有留意的地方。路线约两公里，中途有两处可休息的地点；如果不想走完全程，可在第一处休息点结束。名额十五人，活动免费，但请提前登记，以便准备材料。</p><p><b>p2</b> 带一支笔和愿意观察的心即可，不需要专业相机。工作室会提供简图；愿意的话，也可以画画、写关键词或录一段自己的口头笔记。我们不要求把每个站点都拍下来，更不以照片数量评选“最佳参与者”。</p><p><b>p3</b> 报名时可以说明是否需要大字号路线图。路线信息会提前两天发给大家，其中注明坡道、台阶和休息点。请根据自己的需要判断是否适合；如果某一段不方便通过，联系组织者商量替代路段，不必为了跟上队伍勉强完成。</p><p><b>p4</b> 若预报有较强降雨，工作室会在前一天晚上六点前通知是否改期；没有收到变更通知时，按原计划集合。活动后，参与者可自愿提交一段不超过一百字的观察，用于小册子。未经本人确认，我们不会把你的笔记和姓名一起公开。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 城市慢行工作室｜街角發現計劃
週日上午九點至十一點，從青石廣場出發。我們不比誰走得快，而是一起觀察日常經過卻沒有留意的地方。路線約兩公里，中途有兩處可休息的地點；如果不想走完全程，可在第一處休息點結束。名額十五人，活動免費，但請提前登記，以便準備材料。</p><p><b>p2</b> 帶一支筆和願意觀察的心即可，不需要專業相機。工作室會提供簡圖；願意的話，也可以畫畫、寫關鍵詞或錄一段自己的口頭筆記。我們不要求把每個站點都拍下來，更不以照片數量評選“最佳參與者”。</p><p><b>p3</b> 報名時可以說明是否需要大字號路線圖。路線信息會提前兩天發給大家，其中註明坡道、臺階和休息點。請根據自己的需要判斷是否適合；如果某一段不方便通過，聯繫組織者商量替代路段，不必爲了跟上隊伍勉強完成。</p><p><b>p4</b> 若預報有較強降雨，工作室會在前一天晚上六點前通知是否改期；沒有收到變更通知時，按原計劃集合。活動後，參與者可自願提交一段不超過一百字的觀察，用於小冊子。未經本人確認，我們不會把你的筆記和姓名一起公開。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>46. What would most closely match the purpose of this walk?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Finish all stops before anyone else in the group.</p><p><b>B.</b> Collect more photographs than the other participants.</p><p><b>C.</b> Notice details in familiar surroundings without competing over speed.</p><p><b>D.</b> Use a professional camera to document every point on the route.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>47. A participant expects difficulty with a section containing steps. What does the notice suggest?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Discuss an alternative segment with the organizers.</p><p><b>B.</b> Complete the entire route anyway to avoid delaying the group.</p><p><b>C.</b> Wait until after the event to request any route information.</p><p><b>D.</b> Withdraw automatically without considering other options.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>48. Why is advance registration requested for a free event?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To charge a camera rental fee before departure.</p><p><b>B.</b> To rank applicants according to how fast they can walk.</p><p><b>C.</b> To guarantee that every person will complete the full route.</p><p><b>D.</b> To let the organizers prepare the needed materials.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>49. What should participants do if they receive no rescheduling notice?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Assume any mention of rain automatically cancels the event.</p><p><b>B.</b> Meet according to the original plan.</p><p><b>C.</b> Choose a new date independently and inform the group afterward.</p><p><b>D.</b> Send in their observation notes instead of attending.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>50. Which publication practice is promised for the booklet?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> All registration details will automatically be printed with each note.</p><p><b>B.</b> Only people who use full names may participate in the walk.</p><p><b>C.</b> Publishing a note together with its author’s name requires confirmation.</p><p><b>D.</b> Every participant must contribute exactly one hundred words.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R6 · Questions 51–55</p><h2>A handover that leaves room to learn</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 留言一｜社区工具室管理员赵叔
小宋，下周我外出三天，工具室想请你和两位新志愿者代管。开门时间照旧。借用记录里要写工具名称、借出时间和预计归还日；归还时先核对数量，再放回对应格子。遇到自己不能确认的问题，请记下来联系我，不要凭印象答应。桌上有一本旧记录册，你们可以参考。</p><p><b>p2</b> 留言二｜小宋回复
赵叔，安排收到了。我们看了旧册，发现有些工具写的是简称，新同学分不清“长夹”和“短夹”各放哪里。我想在格子旁加一张名称与照片对照表，再把借用登记的三项要求印在桌面卡片上，这样不用每次都来问您。</p><p><b>p3</b> 不过，旧册上有一件工具已超过原定归还日，旁边只有“等回信”三个字。我们不知道是否已经协商延期，所以先不自行认定借用者违约。我会联系您核实之前的沟通。明天开门前，我们三人想一起走一遍借出和归还流程，请问您是否方便留十分钟带我们练习？如果来不及，我们可以先演练，把不确定的地方集中发给您。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 留言一｜社區工具室管理員趙叔
小宋，下週我外出三天，工具室想請你和兩位新志願者代管。開門時間照舊。借用記錄裏要寫工具名稱、借出時間和預計歸還日；歸還時先覈對數量，再放回對應格子。遇到自己不能確認的問題，請記下來聯繫我，不要憑印象答應。桌上有一本舊記錄冊，你們可以參考。</p><p><b>p2</b> 留言二｜小宋回覆
趙叔，安排收到了。我們看了舊冊，發現有些工具寫的是簡稱，新同學分不清“長夾”和“短夾”各放哪裏。我想在格子旁加一張名稱與照片對照表，再把借用登記的三項要求印在桌面卡片上，這樣不用每次都來問您。</p><p><b>p3</b> 不過，舊冊上有一件工具已超過原定歸還日，旁邊只有“等回信”三個字。我們不知道是否已經協商延期，所以先不自行認定借用者違約。我會聯繫您覈實之前的溝通。明天開門前，我們三人想一起走一遍借出和歸還流程，請問您是否方便留十分鐘帶我們練習？如果來不及，我們可以先演練，把不確定的地方集中發給您。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>51. Which responsibility remains unchanged while Zhao is away?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Volunteers may open the room whenever someone requests it.</p><p><b>B.</b> The room continues to follow its usual opening hours.</p><p><b>C.</b> All borrowed tools must be returned on the day they are borrowed.</p><p><b>D.</b> Only the senior volunteer is allowed to record a loan.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>52. What practical problem would Song’s proposed photo chart address?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The tool room has no available storage spaces.</p><p><b>B.</b> The current borrowing rules require too many return dates.</p><p><b>C.</b> Experienced volunteers are prohibited from naming tools.</p><p><b>D.</b> Abbreviated names are unclear to volunteers unfamiliar with the tools.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>53. Why does Song avoid immediately declaring the overdue borrower at fault?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The note may reflect an extension or communication not yet understood.</p><p><b>B.</b> The tool’s name does not appear anywhere in the old records.</p><p><b>C.</b> Volunteers have been told to disregard all expected return dates.</p><p><b>D.</b> The borrower has already confirmed that the tool was returned.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>54. What does the request for a short joint rehearsal suggest about the volunteers?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> They intend to replace every rule with an informal agreement.</p><p><b>B.</b> They believe written instructions make practice unnecessary.</p><p><b>C.</b> They want to test the process and identify gaps before managing it alone.</p><p><b>D.</b> They will refuse the task unless Zhao remains for all three days.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>55. How do the two messages together portray a useful handover?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It depends on memorizing abbreviations without asking questions.</p><p><b>B.</b> It requires new volunteers to make uncertain judgments immediately.</p><p><b>C.</b> It is complete as soon as the old notebook is placed on the desk.</p><p><b>D.</b> It combines clear procedures, accessible references, practice, and a way to verify uncertainty.</p></div></article></section></main></body></html>